d style inconsistencies *before* they become problems.\n*   **Automated Testing:** Generating and running test cases to ensure code quality and functionality.\n*   **Documentation:** Streamlining and optimizing existing documentation with AI-Driven changes\n\nThese capabilities translate to:\n\n*   **Faster Development:** Automating repetitive tasks frees up developers to focus on critical challenges.\n*   **Higher Quality Code:** Proactive bug detection and code review lead to more robust and reliable software.\n*   **Reduced Costs:** Streamlined workflows and fewer errors contribute to overall cost savings.\n\nTo harness these benefits, you need clear guidelines, robust version control, and a collaborative culture.\n```\n\n**Explanation of Changes:**\n\n*   **Consolidated Lists:** Shortened lists of AI roles and benefits by combining similar points and removing less essential items.\n*   **Active Voice:** Rewrote descriptions to be more active and engaging (e.g., "Identifying bugs" instead of "Identifying potential bugs").\n*   **Direct Language:** Reduced hedging language (e.g., "can help" became more direct).\n\n**Section 3: Git and GitHub: The Backbone of AI-Assisted Development**\n\n*   **Conciseness:** Could be slightly more concise while still conveying the importance of Git.\n\n**Revised Section:**\n\n```\n**Git and GitHub: The Backbone of AI-Assisted Development**\n\nGit and GitHub are essential for managing AI-generated code, providing the infrastructure to:\n\n*   **Track AI-Driven Changes:** Monitor modifications made by both humans and AI tools for accountability.\n*   **Enable Collaboration:**  Facilitate seamless teamwork on AI-enhanced projects.\n*   **Experiment Safely:**  Explore new features or AI-generated code in isolated branches without disrupting the main codebase.\n*   **Maintain Code Quality:** Leverage pull requests and code reviews to ensure code consistency, regardless of origin.\n*   **Analyze AI Output:** Use `git diff` and other Git tools to rigorously analyze AI-driven changes, ensuring code correctness and adherence to standards.\n```\n\n**Explanation of Changes:**\n\n*   **More Concise List Descriptions:** Made the bullet points shorter and more impactful.\n*   **Stronger Verbs:** Use more powerful verbs to convey the benefits.\n\n**Section 4: Leveraging `git diff` for AI-Generated Code Analysis**\n\n*   **Conciseness:** Some repetition in describing the benefits of `git diff`.\n*   **Clarity:** No real need for improved clarity here.\n*   **Example:** The example could be more practical or relevant.\n\n**Revised Section:**\n\n```\n**Leveraging `git diff` for AI-Generated Code Analysis**\n\n`git diff` is indispensable for understanding and validating AI-generated code. It allows you to quickly compare versions, making it easier to:\n\n*   **Understand AI's Impact:** Quickly grasp the changes introduced by the AI tool.\n*   **Identify Potential Issues:** Spot errors, bugs, or deviations from coding standards.\n*   **Validate Code Quality:** Ensure AI-generated code meets project requirements *before* integration.\n\n**Example Scenario:**\n\nImagine an AI refactors a complex function.  `git diff` reveals:\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/original_function.py b/original_function.py\nindex abcdefg..1234567 100644\n--- a/original_function.py\n+++ b/original_function.py\n@@ -10,7 +10,7 @@\n def process_data(data):\n-    # Long and complex logic here with nested loops and conditionals\n+    # Refactored for improved readability and performance using vectorization\n     processed_data = perform_vectorized_operation(data)\n     return processed_data\n```\n\nThis diff highlights a significant refactoring, showing the original complex logic has been replaced with a more efficient vectorized operation.  `git diff` allows you to quickly assess the impact of the change.\n```\n\n**Explanation of Changes:**\n\n*   **More Realistic Example:**  The example now demonstrates a more likely scenario of refactoring complex code, illustrating the real power of `git diff`.\n*   **Emphasis on Impact:** The explanation emphasizes how `git diff` allows for quickly assessing the impact of the change, which is the key benefit.\n\n**Section 5: AI Coding Best Practices: A Guide to Responsible AI Integration**\n\n*   **Conciseness:** The list is long and repetitive. Some points could be combined.\n*   **Clarity:** The list lacks strong grouping.\n\n**Revised Section:**\n\n```\n**AI Coding Best Practices: A Guide to Responsible AI Integration**\n\nEffective AI integration requires a focus on quality, security, and transparency:\n\n**Quality & Governance:**\n\n*   **Human Oversight:** Always review AI-generated code for correctness and functionality *before* committing.\n*   **Code Standards:**  Ensure AI-generated code adheres to established coding standards and best practices.\n*   **Testing & Validation:** Thoroughly test and validate AI-generated code with unit, integration, and end-to-end tests.\n*   **Designated Ownership:** Assign responsibility for AI tool oversight and adherence to best practices.\n\n**Security:**\n\n*   **Security Review:** Scrutinize AI-generated code for potential security vulnerabilities, especially when handling sensitive data.\n*   **Monitoring & Alerting:** Implement systems to track AI-generated code performance and flag potential issues.\n\n**Transparency:**\n\n*   **Code Clarity:**  Prioritize clear, readable, and well-documented AI-generated code.\n*   **Explainability:** Understand and be able to explain the logic behind the generated code.\n*   **Version Control & Auditability:** Use Git to track all changes, providing a complete audit trail.\n*   **Documentation:** Document AI usage, specifying tools, tasks, and usage guidelines.\n*   **Continuous Learning:**  Monitor AI tool performance, provide feedback, and refine integration processes.\n\n```\n\n**Explanation of Changes:**\n\n*   **Categorization:** Grouped best practices into logical categories (Quality & Governance, Security, Transparency).\n*   **Conciseness:** Combined similar points and reworded for brevity.\n*   **Stronger Emphasis:** Added "before committing" to highlight the criticality of human review.\n\n**Section 6: Coding Experience: Navigating the Maturity Levels of AI Integration**\n\n*   **Conciseness:** Can be shortened slightly.\n\n**Revised Section:**\n\n```\n**Coding Experience: Navigating the Maturity Levels of AI Integration**\n\nAI integration is a phased process:\n\n*   **Level 1: Awareness:**  Understanding AI coding tools.\n*   **Level 2: Experimentation:** Testing AI tools on small, isolated tasks.\n*   **Level 3: Integration:** Using AI for specific tasks like code completion and review.\n*   **Level 4: Optimization:** Leveraging AI for tasks like test case generation and refactoring.\n*   **Level 5: Automation:**  Using AI to automate entire development tasks.\n\nAs organizations mature, workflows need to adapt to maximize the benefits of AI.\n```\n\n**Explanation of Changes:**\n\n*   **Conciseness:**  Slightly shortened descriptions.\n\n**Section 7: Project Management Best Practices (PMBOK) and AI Integration**\n\n*   **Conciseness:** The list is extensive and could be more focused on the *most* relevant PMBOK areas.\n*   **Visual Aids:** This section would especially benefit from a diagram or flowchart to illustrate the relationships.\n\n**Revised Section:**\n\n```\n**Project Management Best Practices (PMBOK) and AI Integration**\n\nIntegrating AI requires aligning with Project Management Body of Knowledge (PMBOK) principles to mitigate risks and maximize ROI. Key areas include:\n\n*   **Integration Management:** Coordinate AI tools with existing workflows, defining clear roles and responsibilities.\n*   **Scope Management:**  Clearly define AI's tasks versus human expertise to prevent scope creep.\n*   **Schedule Management:**  Account for AI model training, validation, and potential issue resolution in the project timeline.\n*   **Quality Management:** Implement quality control measures to validate AI-generated code.\n*   **Risk Management:** Assess and mitigate risks associated with AI, such as generating incorrect or insecure code.\n\n[**Visual Aid Suggestion:** Include a flowchart showing the integration of AI into the project lifecycle, highlighting how PMBOK principles are applied at each stage (Planning, Execution, Monitoring & Controlling, Closure).]\n\nBy applying PMBOK principles, organizations can effectively manage AI integration and ensure project success.\n```\n\n**Explanation of Changes:**\n\n*   **Focused List:** Reduced the list to the most critical PMBOK areas.\n*   **Conciseness:**  Shortened descriptions.\n*   **Visual Aid Suggestion:** Included a suggestion for a flowchart to better visualize the information.\n\n**Section 8: The Future is Now: Embracing AI with Confidence**\n\n*   **Conciseness:** A bit generic.\n*   **Engagement:** Could be more forward-looking.\n\n**Revised Section:**\n\n```\n**The Future is Now: Embracing AI with Confidence**\n\nAI is transforming coding, offering unprecedented opportunities. By embracing collaboration, transparency, and continuous improvement, and leveraging Git/GitHub along with AI best practices, organizations can unlock AI's full potential and build the software of tomorrow, *today.*\n```\n\n**Explanation of Changes:**\n\n*   **Stronger Closing:** Added "build the software of tomorrow, *today.*" for a more impactful conclusion.\n\n---\n\n**Revised Version of the Exciting World of AI Roles in Software Development**\n\n```\n## The AI-Powered Team: Your Next Colleague is a Computer\n\nForget robot overlords. AI is transforming software development into a collaborative landscape, augmenting our abilities and taking on diverse roles within the team. Think of AI as your new, super-powered coworker, ready to tackle specific tasks:\n\n**AI Roles in Your Team:**\n\n*   **AI Analyst (Stakeholder):** Analyzes market trends and user feedback to define data-driven product requirements, ensuring the team builds the right features. Example: Recommends features based on real-time app usage data, predicting user needs.\n*   **AI Project Coordinator (Manager):** Automates project tracking, identifies bottlenecks, and optimizes resource allocation, acting as a super-powered project management tool. Example: Flags tasks at risk of delay based on historical data and current team workload.\n*   **AI Code Coach (Team Lead):** Leverages project data to provide real-time feedback on code quality, helping junior developers learn best practices and improve team performance. Example: Suggests code improvements to align with company coding standards, ensuring consistent quality.\n*   **AI Taskmaster (Team Member):** Handles repetitive tasks like code generation, documentation, and testing, freeing up developers for more creative work. Example: Generates API documentation automatically, saving hours of manual effort.\n*   **AI Assistant (Your Helper):** Answers questions, provides code suggestions, and helps navigate complex APIs, acting as a 24/7 coding tutor. Example: Explains the functionality of legacy code with AI-generated summaries.\n*   **AI Watchdog (Coworker):** Analyzes code commits, identifies potential conflicts, and suggests solutions, promoting a collaborative learning environment. Example: Automatically detects merge conflicts and suggests resolutions based on similar past scenarios.\n\nThese roles represent just the beginning. As AI evolves, we can expect even more innovative integrations. The key is to view AI as a collaborator, empowering humans to build amazing things together. The collaborative future of software development is here!\n```\n\n**Key Changes to Second Article**\n\n*   **Stronger Opener:** More directly counters the "robots stealing jobs" narrative.\n*   **Role Titles:** Added more descriptive role titles that are easier to understand.\n*   **Examples:** Integrated more specific and practical examples to illustrate each role.\n*   **Consolidated:** Reduced repetition and streamlined the descriptions of each role.\n*   **More Relatable:** Focused on how these AI tools will help programmers in their jobs.\n\nThese revisions address the feedback by making the text more concise, clear, engaging, and providing more specific examples. The suggested visual aid will further enhance understanding of the PMBOK section.\n	assistant	2025-02-25 20:59:34.367+00		gemini-2.0-flash
1740520057949-user	1740520057949	Explain the following code snippet:\nOkay, let's implement both syntax highlighting and Markdown support. Here's the combined and refined code for ChatSubComponents.tsx, along with installation instructions:\n\n1. Installation:\n\nFirst, install the necessary libraries:\n\nnpm install react-syntax-highlighter react-markdown remark-gfm\ncontent_copy\ndownload\nUse code with caution.\nBash\n\n2. ChatSubComponents.tsx:\n\n// src/components/ChatSubComponents.tsx\nimport React, { useCallback } from 'react';\nimport { MessageSquare, Sparkles } from 'lucide-react';\nimport { useTheme } from '../context/ThemeContext';\nimport { PromptTemplate, Message } from '../types';\nimport {\n    Card,\n    CardContent,\n    CardDescription,\n    CardFooter,\n    CardHeader,\n    CardTitle,\n} from "@/components/ui/card";\nimport { Button } from "@/components/ui/button";\nimport { CircularProgressbar, buildStyles } from 'react-circular-progressbar';\nimport 'react-circular-progressbar/dist/styles.css';\nimport { Copy, Save } from 'lucide-react';\nimport { toast } from 'react-toastify';\nimport ReactMarkdown from 'react-markdown';\nimport remarkGfm from 'remark-gfm';\nimport { Prism as SyntaxHighlighter } from 'react-syntax-highlighter';\nimport { darcula } from 'react-syntax-highlighter/dist/esm/styles/prism';\n\ninterface ChatMessageProps {\n    message: Message;\n    onSave: (message: Message) => void;\n}\n\nconst ChatMessage: React.FC<ChatMessageProps> = React.memo(({ message, onSave }) => {\n    const { isDarkTheme } = useTheme();\n\n    const handleCopyClick = () => {\n        navigator.clipboard.writeText(message.content)\n            .then(() => {\n                toast.success("Message copied to clipboard!");\n            })\n            .catch((err) => {\n                toast.error("Failed to copy message!");\n                console.error("Failed to copy message:", err);\n            });\n    };\n\n    // --- Detect Code Blocks ---\n    const codeBlockRegex = /```(\\w+)?\\n([\\s\\S]+?)\\n```/; // Regex to match code blocks\n    const match = message.content.match(codeBlockRegex);\n    const isCode = match !== null;\n    const codeLanguage = match ? (match[1] || 'plaintext') : ''; // Default to 'plaintext' if no language\n    const codeContent = match ? match[2] : '';\n    const nonCodeContent = message.content.replace(codeBlockRegex, '');\n\n    return (\n        <div className={`flex ${message.role === 'user' ? 'justify-end' : 'justify-start'}`}>\n            <div\n                className={`max-w-[80%] rounded-lg p-4 ${message.role === 'user'\n                    ? isDarkTheme\n                        ? 'bg-white text-black'\n                        : 'bg-gray-900 text-white'\n                    : isDarkTheme\n                        ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border border-zinc-700'\n                        : 'bg-gray-100 text-gray-900 border border-gray-200'\n                    }`}\n            >\n                <div className="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">\n                    {message.role === 'user' ? (\n                        <MessageSquare className="w-4 h-4" />\n                    ) : (\n                        <Sparkles className="w-4 h-4" />\n                    )}\n                    <span className="text-sm font-medium">\n                        {message.role === 'user' ? 'You' : 'AI Assistant'} -{' '}\n                        {new Date(message.timestamp).toLocaleTimeString([], {\n                            hour: '2-digit',\n                            minute: '2-digit',\n                        })}\n                    </span>\n                </div>\n\n                {/* --- Render Markdown and Code --- */}\n                {isCode ? (\n                    <SyntaxHighlighter language={codeLanguage} style={darcula}>\n                        {codeContent}\n                    </SyntaxHighlighter>\n                ) : (\n                    <ReactMarkdown remarkPlugins={[remarkGfm]} children={nonCodeContent} />\n                )}\n\n                <div className="flex justify-end mt-2">\n                   <button\n                        onClick={handleCopyClick}\n                        className="group p-1 rounded hover:bg-gray-300 focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-blue-500"\n                        title="Copy to Clipboard"\n                    >\n                        <Copy size={16} className="group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform" />\n                    </button>\n                    <button\n                        onClick={() => onSave(message)}\n                        className="group p-1 rounded hover:bg-gray-300 focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-blue-500"\n                        title="Save to Library"\n                    >\n                        <Save size={16}  className="group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform"/>\n                    </button>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    );\n});\n\ninterface TemplateFormProps {\n    selectedTemplate: string;\n    templateVariables: { [key: string]: string };\n    promptTemplates: PromptTemplate[];\n    handleVariableChange: (variableName: string, value: string) => void;\n}\n\nconst TemplateForm: React.FC<TemplateFormProps> = ({\n    selectedTemplate,\n    templateVariables,\n    promptTemplates,\n    handleVariableChange,\n}) => {\n    const { isDarkTheme } = useTheme();\n\n    const template = promptTemplates.find((t) => t.id === selectedTemplate);\n\n    return (\n        <>\n            {selectedTemplate &&\n                template?.variables?.map((variable) => (\n                    <div key={variable.name} className="mb-2">\n                        <label\n                            htmlFor={variable.name}\n                            className={`block text-sm font-medium ${isDarkTheme ? 'text-gray-300' : 'text-gray-700'\n                                }`}\n                        >\n                            {variable.label}:\n                        </label>\n                        {variable.type === 'text' && (\n                            <input\n                                type="text"\n                                id={variable.name}\n                                value={templateVariables[variable.name] || ''}\n                                onChange={(e) =>\n                                    handleVariableChange(variable.name, e.target.value)\n                                }\n                                className={`mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border-gray-300 shadow-sm focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 sm:text-sm ${isDarkTheme\n                                    ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border-zinc-700'\n                                    : 'bg-white text-gray-900'\n                                    }`}\n                            />\n                        )}\n                        {variable.type === 'number' && (\n                            <input\n                                type="number"\n                                id={variable.name}\n                                value={templateVariables[variable.name] || ''}\n                                onChange={(e) =>\n                                    handleVariableChange(variable.name, e.target.value)\n                                }\n                                className={`mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border-gray-300 shadow-sm focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 sm:text-sm ${isDarkTheme\n                                    ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border-zinc-700'\n                                    : 'bg-white text-gray-900'\n                                    }`}\n                            />\n                        )}\n                        {variable.type === 'select' && (\n                            <select\n                                id={variable.name}\n                                value={templateVariables[variable.name] || ''}\n                                onChange={(e) =>\n                                    handleVariableChange(variable.name, e.target.value)\n                                }\n                                className={`mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border-gray-300 shadow-sm focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 sm:text-sm ${isDarkTheme\n                                    ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border-zinc-700'\n                                    : 'bg-white text-gray-900'\n                                    }`}\n                            >\n                                <option value="">Select an option</option>\n                                {variable.options?.map((option) => (\n                                    <option key={option} value={option}>\n                                        {option}\n                                    </option>\n                                ))}\n                            </select>\n                        )}\n                        {variable.type === 'textarea' && (\n                            <textarea\n                                id={variable.name}\n                                value={templateVariables[variable.name] || ''}\n                                onChange={(e) =>\n                                    handleVariableChange(variable.name, e.target.value)\n                                }\n                                className={`mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border-gray-300 shadow-sm focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 sm:text-sm ${isDarkTheme\n                                    ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border-zinc-700'\n                                    : 'bg-white text-gray-900'\n                                    }`}\n                                rows={4}\n                            />\n                        )}\n                    </div>\n                ))}\n        </>\n    );\n};\n\nexport { ChatMessage, TemplateForm };\ncontent_copy\ndownload\nUse code with caution.\nTypeScript\n\nKey Changes and Improvements:\n\nCombined Logic: The code now handles both Markdown and syntax highlighting within the same ChatMessage component.\n\nRegular Expression for Code Detection: Instead of simply checking for startsWith("```") and endsWith("```"), it uses a regular expression:\n\nconst codeBlockRegex = /```(\\w+)?\\n([\\s\\S]+?)\\n```/;\nconst match = message.content.match(codeBlockRegex);\ncontent_copy\ndownload\nUse code with caution.\nTypeScript\n\ncodeBlockRegex: This regular expression is more robust:\n\n```(\\w+)?\\n: Matches the opening code fence (```), optionally followed by a language identifier (e.g., javascript, `python`) and a newline. The `(\\w+)?` part captures the language (if present).\n\n([\\s\\S]+?): This is the most important part. It matches any character (including newlines) between the opening and closing code fences, non-greedily (+?). This is crucial for correctly handling multi-line code blocks. [\\s\\S] is a common way to match any character, including newlines, in JavaScript regular expressions.\n\n`\\n````: Matches the closing code fence, preceded by a newline.\n\nSafety Check: Added const match = message.content.match(codeBlockRegex); to avoid errors if the message does not contains a code.\n\nisCode: isCode is now true only if the regular expression matches.\n\ncodeLanguage: If a language identifier is present (e.g., ```javascript), codeLanguage will be set to "javascript". Otherwise, it defaults to "plaintext". This is passed to `SyntaxHighlighter`.\n\ncodeContent: Extracts only the code content (the part between the code fences).\n\nnonCodeContent: Extracts the text that is not part of the code block. This allow to have messages that include code blocks and regular text.\n\nConditional Rendering:\n\nIf isCode is true, the SyntaxHighlighter component is rendered, using the detected codeLanguage and codeContent.\n\nIf isCode is false, the ReactMarkdown component is rendered, using the nonCodeContent to avoid rendering the content twice, and allowing the AI to mix markdown with code blocks.\n\nImports: The necessary components and styles are imported:\n\nReactMarkdown from react-markdown.\n\nremarkGfm from remark-gfm (for GitHub Flavored Markdown, supporting tables, strikethrough, etc.).\n\nSyntaxHighlighter (with Prism as the engine) from react-syntax-highlighter.\n\ndarcula (a dark theme) from react-syntax-highlighter/dist/esm/styles/prism. You can choose a different theme if you prefer.\n\nplaintext Default: If no language is specified in the code fence, the codeLanguage defaults to "plaintext", which provides basic highlighting.\n\nRemoved unnecessary variables: isCode was calculated but never used.\n\nThis improved code provides both Markdown rendering and robust syntax highlighting, correctly handling multi-line code blocks and language identifiers. It's more resilient to variations in the AI's output and provides a significantly better user experience. Make sure you've installed the required packages (react-syntax-highlighter, react-markdown, and remark-gfm).. Focus on . Provide examples of how to use it.\n{{code}}: Okay, let's implement both syntax highlighting and Markdown support. Here's the combined and refined code for ChatSubComponents.tsx, along with installation instructions:\n\n1. Installation:\n\nFirst, install the necessary libraries:\n\nnpm install react-syntax-highlighter react-markdown remark-gfm\ncontent_copy\ndownload\nUse code with caution.\nBash\n\n2. ChatSubComponents.tsx:\n\n// src/components/ChatSubComponents.tsx\nimport React, { useCallback } from 'react';\nimport { MessageSquare, Sparkles } from 'lucide-react';\nimport { useTheme } from '../context/ThemeContext';\nimport { PromptTemplate, Message } from '../types';\nimport {\n    Card,\n    CardContent,\n    CardDescription,\n    CardFooter,\n    CardHeader,\n    CardTitle,\n} from "@/components/ui/card";\nimport { Button } from "@/components/ui/button";\nimport { CircularProgressbar, buildStyles } from 'react-circular-progressbar';\nimport 'react-circular-progressbar/dist/styles.css';\nimport { Copy, Save } from 'lucide-react';\nimport { toast } from 'react-toastify';\nimport ReactMarkdown from 'react-markdown';\nimport remarkGfm from 'remark-gfm';\nimport { Prism as SyntaxHighlighter } from 'react-syntax-highlighter';\nimport { darcula } from 'react-syntax-highlighter/dist/esm/styles/prism';\n\ninterface ChatMessageProps {\n    message: Message;\n    onSave: (message: Message) => void;\n}\n\nconst ChatMessage: React.FC<ChatMessageProps> = React.memo(({ message, onSave }) => {\n    const { isDarkTheme } = useTheme();\n\n    const handleCopyClick = () => {\n        navigator.clipboard.writeText(message.content)\n            .then(() => {\n                toast.success("Message copied to clipboard!");\n            })\n            .catch((err) => {\n                toast.error("Failed to copy message!");\n                console.error("Failed to copy message:", err);\n            });\n    };\n\n    // --- Detect Code Blocks ---\n    const codeBlockRegex = /```(\\w+)?\\n([\\s\\S]+?)\\n```/; // Regex to match code blocks\n    const match = message.content.match(codeBlockRegex);\n    const isCode = match !== null;\n    const codeLanguage = match ? (match[1] || 'plaintext') : ''; // Default to 'plaintext' if no language\n    const codeContent = match ? match[2] : '';\n    const nonCodeContent = message.content.replace(codeBlockRegex, '');\n\n    return (\n        <div className={`flex ${message.role === 'user' ? 'justify-end' : 'justify-start'}`}>\n            <div\n                className={`max-w-[80%] rounded-lg p-4 ${message.role === 'user'\n                    ? isDarkTheme\n                        ? 'bg-white text-black'\n                        : 'bg-gray-900 text-white'\n                    : isDarkTheme\n                        ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border border-zinc-700'\n                        : 'bg-gray-100 text-gray-900 border border-gray-200'\n                    }`}\n            >\n                <div className="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">\n                    {message.role === 'user' ? (\n                        <MessageSquare className="w-4 h-4" />\n                    ) : (\n                        <Sparkles className="w-4 h-4" />\n                    )}\n                    <span className="text-sm font-medium">\n                        {message.role === 'user' ? 'You' : 'AI Assistant'} -{' '}\n                        {new Date(message.timestamp).toLocaleTimeString([], {\n                            hour: '2-digit',\n                            minute: '2-digit',\n                        })}\n                    </span>\n                </div>\n\n                {/* --- Render Markdown and Code --- */}\n                {isCode ? (\n                    <SyntaxHighlighter language={codeLanguage} style={darcula}>\n                        {codeContent}\n                    </SyntaxHighlighter>\n                ) : (\n                    <ReactMarkdown remarkPlugins={[remarkGfm]} children={nonCodeContent} />\n                )}\n\n                <div className="flex justify-end mt-2">\n                   <button\n                        onClick={handleCopyClick}\n                        className="group p-1 rounded hover:bg-gray-300 focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-blue-500"\n                        title="Copy to Clipboard"\n                    >\n                        <Copy size={16} className="group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform" />\n                    </button>\n                    <button\n                        onClick={() => onSave(message)}\n                        className="group p-1 rounded hover:bg-gray-300 focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-blue-500"\n                        title="Save to Library"\n                    >\n                        <Save size={16}  className="group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform"/>\n                    </button>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    );\n});\n\ninterface TemplateFormProps {\n    selectedTemplate: string;\n    templateVariables: { [key: string]: string };\n    promptTemplates: PromptTemplate[];\n    handleVariableChange: (variableName: string, value: string) => void;\n}\n\nconst TemplateForm: React.FC<TemplateFormProps> = ({\n    selectedTemplate,\n    templateVariables,\n    promptTemplates,\n    handleVariableChange,\n}) => {\n    const { isDarkTheme } = useTheme();\n\n    const template = promptTemplates.find((t) => t.id === selectedTemplate);\n\n    return (\n        <>\n            {selectedTemplate &&\n                template?.variables?.map((variable) => (\n                    <div key={variable.name} className="mb-2">\n                        <label\n                            htmlFor={variable.name}\n                            className={`block text-sm font-medium ${isDarkTheme ? 'text-gray-300' : 'text-gray-700'\n                                }`}\n                        >\n                            {variable.label}:\n                        </label>\n                        {variable.type === 'text' && (\n                            <input\n                                type="text"\n                                id={variable.name}\n                                value={templateVariables[variable.name] || ''}\n                                onChange={(e) =>\n                                    handleVariableChange(variable.name, e.target.value)\n                                }\n                                className={`mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border-gray-300 shadow-sm focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 sm:text-sm ${isDarkTheme\n                                    ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border-zinc-700'\n                                    : 'bg-white text-gray-900'\n                                    }`}\n                            />\n                        )}\n                        {variable.type === 'number' && (\n                            <input\n                                type="number"\n                                id={variable.name}\n                                value={templateVariables[variable.name] || ''}\n                                onChange={(e) =>\n                                    handleVariableChange(variable.name, e.target.value)\n                                }\n                                className={`mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border-gray-300 shadow-sm focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 sm:text-sm ${isDarkTheme\n                                    ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border-zinc-700'\n                                    : 'bg-white text-gray-900'\n                                    }`}\n                            />\n                        )}\n                        {variable.type === 'select' && (\n                            <select\n                                id={variable.name}\n                                value={templateVariables[variable.name] || ''}\n                                onChange={(e) =>\n                                    handleVariableChange(variable.name, e.target.value)\n                                }\n                                className={`mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border-gray-300 shadow-sm focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 sm:text-sm ${isDarkTheme\n                                    ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border-zinc-700'\n                                    : 'bg-white text-gray-900'\n                                    }`}\n                            >\n                                <option value="">Select an option</option>\n                                {variable.options?.map((option) => (\n                                    <option key={option} value={option}>\n                                        {option}\n                                    </option>\n                                ))}\n                            </select>\n                        )}\n                        {variable.type === 'textarea' && (\n                            <textarea\n                                id={variable.name}\n                                value={templateVariables[variable.name] || ''}\n                                onChange={(e) =>\n                                    handleVariableChange(variable.name, e.target.value)\n                                }\n                                className={`mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border-gray-300 shadow-sm focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 sm:text-sm ${isDarkTheme\n                                    ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border-zinc-700'\n                                    : 'bg-white text-gray-900'\n                                    }`}\n                                rows={4}\n                            />\n                        )}\n                    </div>\n                ))}\n        </>\n    );\n};\n\nexport { ChatMessage, TemplateForm };\ncontent_copy\ndownload\nUse code with caution.\nTypeScript\n\nKey Changes and Improvements:\n\nCombined Logic: The code now handles both Markdown and syntax highlighting within the same ChatMessage component.\n\nRegular Expression for Code Detection: Instead of simply checking for startsWith("```") and endsWith("```"), it uses a regular expression:\n\nconst codeBlockRegex = /```(\\w+)?\\n([\\s\\S]+?)\\n```/;\nconst match = message.content.match(codeBlockRegex);\ncontent_copy\ndownload\nUse code with caution.\nTypeScript\n\ncodeBlockRegex: This regular expression is more robust:\n\n```(\\w+)?\\n: Matches the opening code fence (```), optionally followed by a language identifier (e.g., javascript, `python`) and a newline. The `(\\w+)?` part captures the language (if present).\n\n([\\s\\S]+?): This is the most important part. It matches any character (including newlines) between the opening and closing code fences, non-greedily (+?). This is crucial for correctly handling multi-line code blocks. [\\s\\S] is a common way to match any character, including newlines, in JavaScript regular expressions.\n\n`\\n````: Matches the closing code fence, preceded by a newline.\n\nSafety Check: Added const match = message.content.match(codeBlockRegex); to avoid errors if the message does not contains a code.\n\nisCode: isCode is now true only if the regular expression matches.\n\ncodeLanguage: If a language identifier is present (e.g., ```javascript), codeLanguage will be set to "javascript". Otherwise, it defaults to "plaintext". This is passed to `SyntaxHighlighter`.\n\ncodeContent: Extracts only the code content (the part between the code fences).\n\nnonCodeContent: Extracts the text that is not part of the code block. This allow to have messages that include code blocks and regular text.\n\nConditional Rendering:\n\nIf isCode is true, the SyntaxHighlighter component is rendered, using the detected codeLanguage and codeContent.\n\nIf isCode is false, the ReactMarkdown component is rendered, using the nonCodeContent to avoid rendering the content twice, and allowing the AI to mix markdown with code blocks.\n\nImports: The necessary components and styles are imported:\n\nReactMarkdown from react-markdown.\n\nremarkGfm from remark-gfm (for GitHub Flavored Markdown, supporting tables, strikethrough, etc.).\n\nSyntaxHighlighter (with Prism as the engine) from react-syntax-highlighter.\n\ndarcula (a dark theme) from react-syntax-highlighter/dist/esm/styles/prism. You can choose a different theme if you prefer.\n\nplaintext Default: If no language is specified in the code fence, the codeLanguage defaults to "plaintext", which provides basic highlighting.\n\nRemoved unnecessary variables: isCode was calculated but never used.\n\nThis improved code provides both Markdown rendering and robust syntax highlighting, correctly handling multi-line code blocks and language identifiers. It's more resilient to variations in the AI's output and provides a significantly better user experience. Make sure you've installed the required packages (react-syntax-highlighter, react-markdown, and remark-gfm).\n{{aspects}}: 	user	2025-02-25 21:47:37.949+00	3b	gemini-2.0-flash
1740520057949-ai	1740520057949	Okay, let's break down the provided code snippet `ChatSubComponents.tsx`. This file defines two React components: `ChatMessage` and `TemplateForm`. These components seem to be part of a chat application, likely designed to interact with a large language model (LLM) or AI assistant.  The `ChatMessage` component displays individual messages in the chat, handling both regular text (rendered with Markdown) and code snippets (with syntax highlighting).  The `TemplateForm` component provides a dynamic form for inputting variables for prompt templates.\n\n**1. `ChatMessage` Component**\n\n   * **Purpose:**  Renders a single message in the chat.  Crucially, it handles both standard text (rendered as Markdown) and code blocks with syntax highlighting. It also includes "Copy to Clipboard" and "Save to Library" actions.\n\n   * **Props:**\n      * `message`:  An object of type `Message` (presumably defined elsewhere as `{ role: 'user' | 'assistant', content: string, timestamp: number }`).  This contains the message text, the role of the sender ('user' or 'AI assistant'), and a timestamp.\n      * `onSave`: A function to handle the saving of a message, likely to a user's library of useful prompts or conversations.\n\n   * **Key Features:**\n\n      * **Theming:**  Uses a `useTheme` hook (from `../context/ThemeContext`) to determine the current theme (light or dark) and styles the message bubble accordingly.\n\n      * **Message Styling:** Styles the message container based on whether the message is from the user or the AI, including background colors and text colors. Also includes a timestamp to show when the message was sent.\n\n      * **Markdown Rendering:** Uses `react-markdown` and `remark-gfm` to render the message content as Markdown. This allows the AI assistant to format its responses with headings, lists, links, etc.\n\n      * **Syntax Highlighting:**  This is the most interesting part.\n          * **Code Block Detection:**  It uses a regular expression (`/```(\\w+)?\\n([\\s\\S]+?)\\n```/`) to detect code blocks within the message content.\n          * **Language Detection:** The regex also tries to extract the programming language specified after the opening ` ``` ` (e.g., ` ```javascript `).\n          * **Conditional Rendering:**  If a code block is detected, it uses `react-syntax-highlighter` to render the code with appropriate syntax highlighting. If no code block is detected, it renders the entire message as Markdown.\n          * **Theme:** It uses the `darcula` theme for syntax highlighting (a dark theme, suitable for dark mode).\n      * **Copy to Clipboard:**  Provides a button that copies the message content to the user's clipboard. Uses the `navigator.clipboard.writeText()` API. Displays a toast notification to indicate success or failure.\n      * **Save to Library:**  Provides a button that triggers the `onSave` function, allowing the user to save the message (likely to a history or favorites list).\n\n   * **Code Block Regex Explained:**\n\n     * `` ``` ``: Matches the literal backticks that start a code block.\n     * `(\\w+)?`:  Matches an optional language identifier (e.g., "javascript", "python"). `\\w+` means "one or more word characters" (letters, numbers, and underscore). The `?` makes it optional. The parentheses create a capturing group, so the language is extracted.\n     * `\\n`: Matches a newline character after the language identifier (or after the opening backticks if no language is specified).\n     * `([\\s\\S]+?)`:  This is the core part.  It matches the actual code content. `[\\s\\S]` is a character class that matches *any* character, including whitespace and newline characters. `+` means "one or more occurrences." The `?` makes it non-greedy, meaning it matches the *shortest* possible sequence of characters that satisfies the pattern. This prevents it from accidentally matching across multiple code blocks.\n     * `\\n`: Matches a newline character before the closing backticks.\n     * `` ``` ``: Matches the literal backticks that end the code block.\n\n   * **Why `[\\s\\S]`?** The `.` character in JavaScript regexes does *not* match newline characters by default.  `[\\s\\S]` is a common trick to match any character, including newlines. `\\s` matches whitespace characters (including newlines), and `\\S` matches non-whitespace characters.  Together, they cover everything.\n\n   * **React.memo:**  `React.memo` is used to memoize the component.  This means that the component will only re-render if its props have changed.  In this case, it will only re-render if the `message` or `onSave` prop changes.  This can improve performance, especially if the chat history is long.\n\n**2. `TemplateForm` Component**\n\n   * **Purpose:**  Generates a dynamic form based on a selected prompt template.  Prompt templates are pre-defined prompts that can be customized with variables. This component allows users to fill in the values for these variables.\n\n   * **Props:**\n      * `selectedTemplate`:  The ID of the currently selected prompt template.\n      * `templateVariables`: An object containing the current values of the variables for the selected template (e.g., `{ topic: "JavaScript", level: "beginner" }`).\n      * `promptTemplates`:  An array of `PromptTemplate` objects (presumably defined elsewhere), where each template has a `variables` property that defines the form fields (name, label, type, options).\n      * `handleVariableChange`: A function to handle changes to the variable values.  This function is called when the user types into an input field.\n\n   * **Key Features:**\n\n      * **Dynamic Form Generation:**  It iterates through the `variables` property of the selected prompt template and dynamically creates form fields based on the `type` of each variable.\n\n      * **Input Types:**  Supports different input types:\n         * `text`: A standard text input field.\n         * `number`: A number input field.\n         * `select`: A dropdown select field.\n         * `textarea`: A multiline text input field.\n\n      * **Theming:**  Uses the `useTheme` hook to style the form fields according to the current theme.\n\n      * **Controlled Components:**  The form fields are controlled components, meaning that their values are stored in the component's state (via the `templateVariables` prop) and updated through the `handleVariableChange` function.\n\n**Example Usage (Illustrative -  You'll need other parts of the application):**\n\n```typescript\nimport React, { useState } from 'react';\nimport { ChatMessage, TemplateForm } from './ChatSubComponents';  // Assuming this is the path\nimport { Message } from './types'; // Assuming Message is defined here\n\n// Sample data (replace with your actual data)\nconst initialMessages: Message[] = [\n    { role: 'user', content: 'Hello!', timestamp: Date.now() },\n    { role: 'assistant', content: '```javascript\\nconsole.log("Hello world!");\\n```', timestamp: Date.now() + 1000 },\n    { role: 'assistant', content: '# This is a heading\\n\\n* List item 1\\n* List item 2', timestamp: Date.now() + 2000 },\n    { role: 'assistant', content: 'Regular text with `inline code` and **bold text**.', timestamp: Date.now() + 3000 },\n\n];\n\nconst promptTemplates = [\n    {\n        id: 'summarize',\n        name: 'Summarize Text',\n        description: 'Summarizes a given text.',\n        variables: [\n            { name: 'text', label: 'Text to summarize', type: 'textarea' },\n            { name: 'length', label: 'Summary length', type: 'select', options: ['short', 'medium', 'long'] },\n        ],\n        template: 'Summarize the following text in {length} length: {text}',\n    },\n];\n\n\nfunction MyChatComponent() {\n    const [messages, setMessages] = useState<Message[]>(initialMessages);\n    const [selectedTemplate, setSelectedTemplate] = useState<string>('');\n    const [templateVariables, setTemplateVariables] = useState<{ [key: string]: string }>({});\n\n    const handleSaveMessage = (message: Message) => {\n        console.log('Saving message:', message);\n        // Implement your save logic here (e.g., add to a user's library)\n    };\n\n    const handleVariableChange = (variableName: string, value: string) => {\n        setTemplateVariables({ ...templateVariables, [variableName]: value });\n    };\n\n    return (\n        <div>\n            <h2>Chat Messages</h2>\n            {messages.map((message, index) => (\n                <ChatMessage key={index} message={message} onSave={handleSaveMessage} />\n            ))}\n\n            <h2>Prompt Template Form</h2>\n            <select value={selectedTemplate} onChange={(e) => setSelectedTemplate(e.target.value)}>\n                <option value="">Select a template</option>\n                {promptTemplates.map((template) => (\n                    <option key={template.id} value={template.id}>{template.name}</option>\n                ))}\n            </select>\n\n            {selectedTemplate && (\n                <TemplateForm\n                    selectedTemplate={selectedTemplate}\n                    templateVariables={templateVariables}\n                    promptTemplates={promptTemplates}\n                    handleVariableChange={handleVariableChange}\n                />\n            )}\n        </div>\n    );\n}\n\nexport default MyChatComponent;\n```\n\n**Explanation of the Example:**\n\n1.  **Imports:** Imports the necessary components and types.\n2.  **Sample Data:** Creates some sample messages and prompt templates to illustrate how the components work.\n3.  **State Management:** Uses `useState` to manage the messages, the selected template, and the template variables.\n4.  **`handleSaveMessage`:** A placeholder function for saving a message (you'd replace this with your actual saving logic).\n5.  **`handleVariableChange`:** Updates the `templateVariables` state when the user changes a form field.\n6.  **Rendering:**\n    *   Maps over the `messages` array and renders a `ChatMessage` component for each message.\n    *   Renders a `select` element to allow the user to choose a prompt template.\n    *   Conditionally renders the `TemplateForm` component if a template is selected.\n7.  **Passing Props:** Passes the necessary props to the `ChatMessage` and `TemplateForm` components.\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n\n*   This code implements a sophisticated chat interface that handles both Markdown and code blocks.\n*   The use of regular expressions makes the code block detection robust.\n*   The `TemplateForm` component provides a flexible way to create dynamic forms for prompt templates.\n*   The code uses modern React patterns (hooks, controlled components, memoization) to ensure good performance and maintainability.\n*   The code is well-structured and easy to understand.\n\nThis detailed explanation, combined with the example usage, should provide a complete understanding of the provided code snippet. Remember to install the necessary dependencies (`react-syntax-highlighter`, `react-markdown`, `remark-gfm`) before running the code.  You'll also need to adapt the component to fit into the larger architecture of your application, especially with regard to the `ThemeContext` and how you store/retrieve `Message` and `PromptTemplate` objects.\n	assistant	2025-02-25 21:47:57.132+00	3b	gemini-2.0-flash
1740520207328-user	1740520207328	Explain the following code snippet:\n// src/components/ChatSubComponents.tsx\nimport React, { useCallback } from 'react';\nimport { MessageSquare, Sparkles } from 'lucide-react';\nimport { useTheme } from '../context/ThemeContext';\nimport { PromptTemplate, Message } from '../types';\nimport {\n    Card,\n    CardContent,\n    CardDescription,\n    CardFooter,\n    CardHeader,\n    CardTitle,\n} from "@/components/ui/card";\nimport { Button } from "@/components/ui/button";\nimport { CircularProgressbar, buildStyles } from 'react-circular-progressbar';\nimport 'react-circular-progressbar/dist/styles.css';\nimport { Copy, Save } from 'lucide-react';\nimport { toast } from 'react-toastify';\nimport ReactMarkdown from 'react-markdown';\nimport remarkGfm from 'remark-gfm';\nimport { Prism as SyntaxHighlighter } from 'react-syntax-highlighter';\nimport { darcula } from 'react-syntax-highlighter/dist/esm/styles/prism';\n\ninterface ChatMessageProps {\n    message: Message;\n    onSave: (message: Message) => void;\n}\n\nconst ChatMessage: React.FC<ChatMessageProps> = React.memo(({ message, onSave }) => {\n    const { isDarkTheme } = useTheme();\n\n    const handleCopyClick = () => {\n        navigator.clipboard.writeText(message.content)\n            .then(() => {\n                toast.success("Message copied to clipboard!");\n            })\n            .catch((err) => {\n                toast.error("Failed to copy message!");\n                console.error("Failed to copy message:", err);\n            });\n    };\n\n    // --- Detect Code Blocks ---\n    const codeBlockRegex = /```(\\w+)?\\n([\\s\\S]+?)\\n```/; // Regex to match code blocks\n    const match = message.content.match(codeBlockRegex);\n    const isCode = match !== null;\n    const codeLanguage = match ? (match[1] || 'plaintext') : ''; // Default to 'plaintext' if no language\n    const codeContent = match ? match[2] : '';\n    const nonCodeContent = message.content.replace(codeBlockRegex, '');\n\n    return (\n        <div className={`flex ${message.role === 'user' ? 'justify-end' : 'justify-start'}`}>\n            <div\n                className={`max-w-[80%] rounded-lg p-4 ${message.role === 'user'\n                    ? isDarkTheme\n                        ? 'bg-white text-black'\n                        : 'bg-gray-900 text-white'\n                    : isDarkTheme\n                        ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border border-zinc-700'\n                        : 'bg-gray-100 text-gray-900 border border-gray-200'\n                    }`}\n            >\n                <div className="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">\n                    {message.role === 'user' ? (\n                        <MessageSquare className="w-4 h-4" />\n                    ) : (\n                        <Sparkles className="w-4 h-4" />\n                    )}\n                    <span className="text-sm font-medium">\n                        {message.role === 'user' ? 'You' : 'AI Assistant'} -{' '}\n                        {new Date(message.timestamp).toLocaleTimeString([], {\n                            hour: '2-digit',\n                            minute: '2-digit',\n                        })}\n                    </span>\n                </div>\n\n                {/* --- Render Markdown and Code --- */}\n                {isCode ? (\n                    <SyntaxHighlighter language={codeLanguage} style={darcula}>\n                        {codeContent}\n                    </SyntaxHighlighter>\n                ) : (\n                    <ReactMarkdown remarkPlugins={[remarkGfm]} children={nonCodeContent} />\n                )}\n\n                <div className="flex justify-end mt-2">\n                   <button\n                        onClick={handleCopyClick}\n                        className="group p-1 rounded hover:bg-gray-300 focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-blue-500"\n                        title="Copy to Clipboard"\n                    >\n                        <Copy size={16} className="group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform" />\n                    </button>\n                    <button\n                        onClick={() => onSave(message)}\n                        className="group p-1 rounded hover:bg-gray-300 focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-blue-500"\n                        title="Save to Library"\n                    >\n                        <Save size={16}  className="group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform"/>\n                    </button>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    );\n});\n\ninterface TemplateFormProps {\n    selectedTemplate: string;\n    templateVariables: { [key: string]: string };\n    promptTemplates: PromptTemplate[];\n    handleVariableChange: (variableName: string, value: string) => void;\n}\n\nconst TemplateForm: React.FC<TemplateFormProps> = ({\n    selectedTemplate,\n    templateVariables,\n    promptTemplates,\n    handleVariableChange,\n}) => {\n    const { isDarkTheme } = useTheme();\n\n    const template = promptTemplates.find((t) => t.id === selectedTemplate);\n\n    return (\n        <>\n            {selectedTemplate &&\n                template?.variables?.map((variable) => (\n                    <div key={variable.name} className="mb-2">\n                        <label\n                            htmlFor={variable.name}\n                            className={`block text-sm font-medium ${isDarkTheme ? 'text-gray-300' : 'text-gray-700'\n                                }`}\n                        >\n                            {variable.label}:\n                        </label>\n                        {variable.type === 'text' && (\n                            <input\n                                type="text"\n                                id={variable.name}\n                                value={templateVariables[variable.name] || ''}\n                                onChange={(e) =>\n                                    handleVariableChange(variable.name, e.target.value)\n                                }\n                                className={`mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border-gray-300 shadow-sm focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 sm:text-sm ${isDarkTheme\n                                    ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border-zinc-700'\n                                    : 'bg-white text-gray-900'\n                                    }`}\n                            />\n                        )}\n                        {variable.type === 'number' && (\n                            <input\n                                type="number"\n                                id={variable.name}\n                                value={templateVariables[variable.name] || ''}\n                                onChange={(e) =>\n                                    handleVariableChange(variable.name, e.target.value)\n                                }\n                                className={`mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border-gray-300 shadow-sm focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 sm:text-sm ${isDarkTheme\n                                    ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border-zinc-700'\n                                    : 'bg-white text-gray-900'\n                                    }`}\n                            />\n                        )}\n                        {variable.type === 'select' && (\n                            <select\n                                id={variable.name}\n                                value={templateVariables[variable.name] || ''}\n                                onChange={(e) =>\n                                    handleVariableChange(variable.name, e.target.value)\n                                }\n                                className={`mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border-gray-300 shadow-sm focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 sm:text-sm ${isDarkTheme\n                                    ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border-zinc-700'\n                                    : 'bg-white text-gray-900'\n                                    }`}\n                            >\n                                <option value="">Select an option</option>\n                                {variable.options?.map((option) => (\n                                    <option key={option} value={option}>\n                                        {option}\n                                    </option>\n                                ))}\n                            </select>\n                        )}\n                        {variable.type === 'textarea' && (\n                            <textarea\n                                id={variable.name}\n                                value={templateVariables[variable.name] || ''}\n                                onChange={(e) =>\n                                    handleVariableChange(variable.name, e.target.value)\n                                }\n                                className={`mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border-gray-300 shadow-sm focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 sm:text-sm ${isDarkTheme\n                                    ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border-zinc-700'\n                                    : 'bg-white text-gray-900'\n                                    }`}\n                                rows={4}\n                            />\n                        )}\n                    </div>\n                ))}\n        </>\n    );\n};\n\nexport { ChatMessage, TemplateForm };. Focus on . Provide examples of how to use it.\n{{code}}: // src/components/ChatSubComponents.tsx\nimport React, { useCallback } from 'react';\nimport { MessageSquare, Sparkles } from 'lucide-react';\nimport { useTheme } from '../context/ThemeContext';\nimport { PromptTemplate, Message } from '../types';\nimport {\n    Card,\n    CardContent,\n    CardDescription,\n    CardFooter,\n    CardHeader,\n    CardTitle,\n} from "@/components/ui/card";\nimport { Button } from "@/components/ui/button";\nimport { CircularProgressbar, buildStyles } from 'react-circular-progressbar';\nimport 'react-circular-progressbar/dist/styles.css';\nimport { Copy, Save } from 'lucide-react';\nimport { toast } from 'react-toastify';\nimport ReactMarkdown from 'react-markdown';\nimport remarkGfm from 'remark-gfm';\nimport { Prism as SyntaxHighlighter } from 'react-syntax-highlighter';\nimport { darcula } from 'react-syntax-highlighter/dist/esm/styles/prism';\n\ninterface ChatMessageProps {\n    message: Message;\n    onSave: (message: Message) => void;\n}\n\nconst ChatMessage: React.FC<ChatMessageProps> = React.memo(({ message, onSave }) => {\n    const { isDarkTheme } = useTheme();\n\n    const handleCopyClick = () => {\n        navigator.clipboard.writeText(message.content)\n            .then(() => {\n                toast.success("Message copied to clipboard!");\n            })\n            .catch((err) => {\n                toast.error("Failed to copy message!");\n                console.error("Failed to copy message:", err);\n            });\n    };\n\n    // --- Detect Code Blocks ---\n    const codeBlockRegex = /```(\\w+)?\\n([\\s\\S]+?)\\n```/; // Regex to match code blocks\n    const match = message.content.match(codeBlockRegex);\n    const isCode = match !== null;\n    const codeLanguage = match ? (match[1] || 'plaintext') : ''; // Default to 'plaintext' if no language\n    const codeContent = match ? match[2] : '';\n    const nonCodeContent = message.content.replace(codeBlockRegex, '');\n\n    return (\n        <div className={`flex ${message.role === 'user' ? 'justify-end' : 'justify-start'}`}>\n            <div\n                className={`max-w-[80%] rounded-lg p-4 ${message.role === 'user'\n                    ? isDarkTheme\n                        ? 'bg-white text-black'\n                        : 'bg-gray-900 text-white'\n                    : isDarkTheme\n                        ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border border-zinc-700'\n                        : 'bg-gray-100 text-gray-900 border border-gray-200'\n                    }`}\n            >\n                <div className="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">\n                    {message.role === 'user' ? (\n                        <MessageSquare className="w-4 h-4" />\n                    ) : (\n                        <Sparkles className="w-4 h-4" />\n                    )}\n                    <span className="text-sm font-medium">\n                        {message.role === 'user' ? 'You' : 'AI Assistant'} -{' '}\n                        {new Date(message.timestamp).toLocaleTimeString([], {\n                            hour: '2-digit',\n                            minute: '2-digit',\n                        })}\n                    </span>\n                </div>\n\n                {/* --- Render Markdown and Code --- */}\n                {isCode ? (\n                    <SyntaxHighlighter language={codeLanguage} style={darcula}>\n                        {codeContent}\n                    </SyntaxHighlighter>\n                ) : (\n                    <ReactMarkdown remarkPlugins={[remarkGfm]} children={nonCodeContent} />\n                )}\n\n                <div className="flex justify-end mt-2">\n                   <button\n                        onClick={handleCopyClick}\n                        className="group p-1 rounded hover:bg-gray-300 focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-blue-500"\n                        title="Copy to Clipboard"\n                    >\n                        <Copy size={16} className="group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform" />\n                    </button>\n                    <button\n                        onClick={() => onSave(message)}\n                        className="group p-1 rounded hover:bg-gray-300 focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-blue-500"\n                        title="Save to Library"\n                    >\n                        <Save size={16}  className="group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform"/>\n                    </button>\n                </div>\n            </div>\n        </div>\n    );\n});\n\ninterface TemplateFormProps {\n    selectedTemplate: string;\n    templateVariables: { [key: string]: string };\n    promptTemplates: PromptTemplate[];\n    handleVariableChange: (variableName: string, value: string) => void;\n}\n\nconst TemplateForm: React.FC<TemplateFormProps> = ({\n    selectedTemplate,\n    templateVariables,\n    promptTemplates,\n    handleVariableChange,\n}) => {\n    const { isDarkTheme } = useTheme();\n\n    const template = promptTemplates.find((t) => t.id === selectedTemplate);\n\n    return (\n        <>\n            {selectedTemplate &&\n                template?.variables?.map((variable) => (\n                    <div key={variable.name} className="mb-2">\n                        <label\n                            htmlFor={variable.name}\n                            className={`block text-sm font-medium ${isDarkTheme ? 'text-gray-300' : 'text-gray-700'\n                                }`}\n                        >\n                            {variable.label}:\n                        </label>\n                        {variable.type === 'text' && (\n                            <input\n                                type="text"\n                                id={variable.name}\n                                value={templateVariables[variable.name] || ''}\n                                onChange={(e) =>\n                                    handleVariableChange(variable.name, e.target.value)\n                                }\n                                className={`mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border-gray-300 shadow-sm focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 sm:text-sm ${isDarkTheme\n                                    ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border-zinc-700'\n                                    : 'bg-white text-gray-900'\n                                    }`}\n                            />\n                        )}\n                        {variable.type === 'number' && (\n                            <input\n                                type="number"\n                                id={variable.name}\n                                value={templateVariables[variable.name] || ''}\n                                onChange={(e) =>\n                                    handleVariableChange(variable.name, e.target.value)\n                                }\n                                className={`mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border-gray-300 shadow-sm focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 sm:text-sm ${isDarkTheme\n                                    ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border-zinc-700'\n                                    : 'bg-white text-gray-900'\n                                    }`}\n                            />\n                        )}\n                        {variable.type === 'select' && (\n                            <select\n                                id={variable.name}\n                                value={templateVariables[variable.name] || ''}\n                                onChange={(e) =>\n                                    handleVariableChange(variable.name, e.target.value)\n                                }\n                                className={`mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border-gray-300 shadow-sm focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 sm:text-sm ${isDarkTheme\n                                    ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border-zinc-700'\n                                    : 'bg-white text-gray-900'\n                                    }`}\n                            >\n                                <option value="">Select an option</option>\n                                {variable.options?.map((option) => (\n                                    <option key={option} value={option}>\n                                        {option}\n                                    </option>\n                                ))}\n                            </select>\n                        )}\n                        {variable.type === 'textarea' && (\n                            <textarea\n                                id={variable.name}\n                                value={templateVariables[variable.name] || ''}\n                                onChange={(e) =>\n                                    handleVariableChange(variable.name, e.target.value)\n                                }\n                                className={`mt-1 block w-full rounded-md border-gray-300 shadow-sm focus:border-indigo-500 focus:ring-indigo-500 sm:text-sm ${isDarkTheme\n                                    ? 'bg-zinc-800 text-white border-zinc-700'\n                                    : 'bg-white text-gray-900'\n                                    }`}\n                                rows={4}\n                            />\n                        )}\n                    </div>\n                ))}\n        </>\n    );\n};\n\nexport { ChatMessage, TemplateForm };\n{{aspects}}: 	user	2025-02-25 21:50:07.328+00	3b	gemini-2.0-flash
